Vaultwarden on Proxmox

Notes for running Vaultwarden, a self-hosted, Bitwarden-compatible password manager, as an LXC container on Proxmox.

Based on this video by Barmine Tech: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cym-wN9yACU

Placeholders used in this file: example.com is my domain, 192.168.1.82 is the Vaultwarden container, 192.168.1.80 is Nginx Proxy Manager.

What it is
Bitwarden is an online password manager. Vaultwarden is a lightweight self-hosted version of it.
You keep full control of your passwords, since they are stored on your own server, encrypted.
It supports multi-factor authentication, including hardware security keys.
The project is actively maintained and security updates are released often, so keep it updated.
Where to host it
Option	Notes
Public (VPS)	Reachable everywhere, but exposed to the internet, so people may try to break in.
At home, opened to the internet	Needs ports 80 or 443 open, which many home ISPs do not allow.
At home, internal only	Reach it from outside through a VPN (such as Tailscale). Safest option.

This setup is internal only. Your vault holds passwords and possibly card and finance details, so do not expose it to the public internet.

Prerequisite: HTTPS

Vaultwarden only works properly over HTTPS. Creating an account over plain HTTP fails with an error saying HTTPS is required. You need a reverse proxy with an SSL certificate in front of it. See ../nginx-proxy-manager/README.md.

1. Install (Proxmox helper script)
Open the Proxmox VE Helper-Scripts website, go to View Scripts and search for Vaultwarden (it is also under Miscellaneous). The page shows the default specs, a link to the project and the script source.
Copy the install command.
In Proxmox, right-click the node, open a Shell, paste the command and press Enter.
Confirm that you want to create a new Vaultwarden container.
Choose Default settings, or Advanced settings to change the container ID, hostname, hardware or the Debian/Ubuntu or Alpine build.
Wait. The container build can take 10 to 15 minutes and may sit on "creating Vaultwarden... patience", which is normal.
When it finishes, it prints the container's address and URL (port 8000).

Review a helper script before running it, because it runs as root on the Proxmox host.

2. Reserve the address

Reserve the container's address on the router so the proxy target never changes:

192.168.1.82   Vaultwarden

Check the address from the Proxmox host:

pct list
pct exec 100 ip a
3. Add a proxy host in Nginx Proxy Manager

In NPM (http://192.168.1.80:81), go to Hosts > Proxy Hosts > Add Proxy Host.

Setting	Value
Domain Names	vault.example.com (click to add it)
Scheme	http or https, whichever loads (mine uses https)
Forward Hostname / IP	192.168.1.82
Forward Port	8000
Cache Assets	On (the video turns it on)
Websockets Support	On
Block Common Exploits	On
SSL tab	Select the wildcard certificate
Force SSL	On

Save, then open https://vault.example.com. It shows a login page.

If it does not load, edit the host and flip the scheme between http and https.

4. Create your account

Vaultwarden has no default credentials. Create your own:

Click Create account on the login page.
Enter an email address, a name and a master password.
If you see a red error saying HTTPS is required, you are not using the HTTPS name. Use https://vault.example.com, not the IP address.
Log in and choose Not now when the browser offers to save the password.

The master password protects everything in the vault. Make it strong and something you will remember, because it is the only password into your instance and it cannot be recovered.

5. Import existing passwords (optional)
In your current browser's password manager, export the passwords to a file (usually JSON or CSV).
In Vaultwarden, go to Tools > Import data and import the file.
Delete the exported file afterwards. It contains your passwords in plain text.
6. Browser extension and phone app

Browser extension:

Install the Bitwarden extension from your browser's store.
Before logging in, choose the self-hosted option (the settings icon on the login screen).
Set the server URL to https://vault.example.com and save.
Log in with your email and master password.

Phone app:

Install the standard Bitwarden app (Android or iPhone).
On the login screen, set the self-hosted server URL to https://vault.example.com.
Log in.

With my setup the name only resolves for devices on my Tailscale network, so turn Tailscale on first when away from home.

7. Using it
My Vault stores logins, cards, identities and secure notes.
With the extension installed, it offers to save new logins and autofills saved ones.
Generator: use long, strong passwords (the video uses at least 25 characters).
Send shares credentials or notes temporarily.
Reports show weak, reused or exposed passwords.
8. Settings worth checking
Preferences: dark mode, language and the vault timeout (how long before the vault locks and asks you to log in again).
Security: change the master password, turn on two-factor authentication, view encryption keys.
Emergency access: let a trusted person request access to the vault if something happens to you.
9. Back up the vault
Go to Tools > Export vault and choose JSON.
Store the file somewhere safe and offline. It contains every login, URL and password in plain text.
Importing the file into a new Vaultwarden instance restores everything.

Also back up the container with a Proxmox backup, and keep that backup somewhere other than the same machine.

10. Security notes
Keep it internal. Do not port-forward ports 80 or 443 to it.
Reach it from outside only through a VPN. Here, Tailscale gives the NPM container a private address, so only my own devices can open the site.
Turn on two-factor authentication for the vault, Cloudflare, Proxmox and Tailscale.
Update Vaultwarden regularly. It stores sensitive data, and updates include security fixes.
Never commit an exported vault, passwords, API tokens or .env files to GitHub.
Troubleshooting
Symptom	Cause and fix
Red error: HTTPS required	Open the site through the proxy name (https://vault.example.com), not http://IP:8000.
Page will not load through the proxy	Flip the scheme between http and https, and check the IP and port 8000.
502 Bad Gateway	NPM cannot reach 192.168.1.82:8000. Check the container is running and the address has not changed.
504 or timeout	Tailscale is off on the device, or the NPM container is offline.
Name does not resolve	DNS still propagating, or the Cloudflare records are wrong. See the NPM README.
Extension or app will not log in	The server URL is wrong, or still set to bitwarden.com. Set the self-hosted URL.
Certificate warning	The wildcard certificate is not selected on the proxy host's SSL tab.
